Yes—if by selective pressure you mean breeding across generations, not physically stressing one plant.

If taller banana plants are the ones preferentially propagated because height confers some advantage, then over generations you can shift the population toward greater height. In simplified form:

\text{variation} + \text{selection} + \text{inheritance} \rightarrow \text{population shift}

The interesting part is that selection never acts on “height” alone. Taller plants may also bring tradeoffs: wind vulnerability, lodging, harvest difficulty, energy allocation, time to fruit, disease exposure, etc. So what you really select is a bundle of correlated traits.

And because many cultivated bananas are clonally propagated, intentional human selection can be unusually strong: once you find a desirable phenotype, you can copy it repeatedly rather than waiting for ordinary sexual reproduction.

So yes, the broader operator is:

Apply pressure repeatedly → amplify traits that survive or are chosen under that pressure.

That works for organisms, behaviors, institutions, algorithms, cultures, even stories. The pressure defines what becomes more common—but sometimes with hidden side effects because you are selecting the whole system, not just the visible trait.